  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066605/assets/fb8d76e5fb2e83676fbab4178b523bf8cbff076490f12fa8be17c37ce06e1820.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Determine the **expanded form** of $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,100$",
  "worked_solution": "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1,2}=\\pm 2,$ and ${x}_{3,4}=\\pm 5\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=1.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =1(x-2)(x-(-2))(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(x-2)(x+2)(x-5\\text{i})(x+5\\text{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)(x+2)(x-5\\text{i})(x+5\\text{i}) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2x-2x-4)({x}^{2}+5\\text{i}x-5\\text{i}x-(5\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4)({x}^{2}+25) & = \\\\ {x}^{4}+25{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}-100 & = \\\\ {x}^{4}+21{x}^{2}-100 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)={x}^{4}+21{x}^{2}-100.$",
